    "item_id": "manual:demo-2026-11-chatbot-ruling",
    "event": "A (fictional) state appellate court held a retailer liable for refund promises",
    "event_description": "A state appellate court (the item marks it as fictional, a manually entered demo) held a retailer liable for refund promises its customer-service chatbot made. The court rejected the defense that the chatbot was a separate agent responsible for its own statements, and it let affected customers pursue their claims together as a group.",
    "source": "manual",
    "source_url": "https://example.com/fictional-ruling",
    "event_date": "2026-11-20",
    "evidence_type": "court_ruling",
    "parameter": "perils.unauthorized_commitment.severity.tail.prob",
    "old": {
     "min": 0.0005,
     "mode": 0.002,
     "max": 0.006
    },
    "new": {
     "min": 0.0005196,
     "mode": 0.002078,
     "max": 0.006235
    },
    "mode_change_pct": 0.038999999999999924,
    "implied_change": 0.3,
    "strength": "moderate",
    "prior_weight": 20.0,
    "evidence_weight": 3.0,
    "reasoning": "This is directly on point for unauthorized_commitment. Liability for chatbot promises is already reflected in the prior through the Moffatt v. Air Canada reference, so the new element is that group claims were allowed. A systematic chatbot promise made to many customers can now aggregate into a group claim. That points to a higher share of claims becoming group claims, so I propose a +30% change to tail.prob. A possible secondary effect is a higher p_claim_given_harm, but only one parameter can be moved and the group-claim holding is the more novel signal. An appellate ruling would normally rate as strong. I rated it moderate because the item is labeled fictional or demo, comes from a single jurisdiction, and gives no loss figures."
   },
